Here's what she said.

1856
01:09:39.960 --> 01:09:50.300
It is true that while I was building my acting career and, you know, doing pretty well, that I kept my side job at Cal State Long Beach.

1857
01:09:50.300 --> 01:09:54.920
I was an assistant editor for a social work journal.

1858
01:09:55.180 --> 01:10:05.040
I did that for 12 years, all through Reno 911, all through, you know, all these movies that I did, Rules of Engagement, and right up until the Goldbergs.

1859
01:10:05.160 --> 01:10:09.800
And the only reason I'm not doing it anymore is because the journal folded.

1860
01:10:09.800 --> 01:10:14.000
It went out of business, but it was a great job.

1861
01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:16.560
It was, like, not even 20 hours a week.

1862
01:10:16.560 --> 01:10:18.820
I could do it from wherever I was.

1863
01:10:18.940 --> 01:10:22.500
And it was all about, you know, people in the social work profession.

1864
01:10:22.500 --> 01:10:27.140
And let's be honest, those people deserve a lot of credit for what they do.

1865
01:10:27.280 --> 01:10:28.560
They don't get recognized enough.

1866
01:10:28.980 --> 01:10:29.920
Yeah, it was great.

1867
01:10:29.920 --> 01:10:31.440
And I loved doing it.

1868
01:10:31.520 --> 01:10:36.820
I loved my original boss who used to come and see my shows at the Groundlings.

1869
01:10:36.820 --> 01:10:40.900
Like, she was just so super supportive that she said, please don't ever quit.

1870
01:10:40.900 --> 01:10:44.780
So I kept my word, and I stayed until the very end.

1871
01:10:45.320 --> 01:10:47.760
See what I was saying about amazing human being?

1872
01:10:48.000 --> 01:10:48.880
I love her.

1873
01:10:49.160 --> 01:10:51.380
You know, that was so moving to me when I heard it.

1874
01:10:51.600 --> 01:10:53.220
My niece is a social worker.

1875
01:10:54.120 --> 01:11:01.480
I'm just always, you know, honestly, guys, I'm lifting her up in prayer all the time because it's not an easy job.

1876
01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:05.800
And I'm thankful that we have people that have a passion for helping others in that way.

1877
01:11:05.800 --> 01:11:11.400
Yeah, it's not an easy job because sometimes you have to see and confront real hard things.

1878
01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:11.760
Yes.

1879
01:11:12.180 --> 01:11:13.900
And then you have to hold that.

1880
01:11:14.200 --> 01:11:14.460
Yeah.

1881
01:11:15.040 --> 01:11:15.300
Yeah.

1882
01:11:15.820 --> 01:11:25.060
Well, as if you needed another reason to love Wendy, I would also like to share that in 2009, 2010, and 2011,

1883
01:11:25.840 --> 01:11:30.920
she rang in the New Year, performing at military bases in Iraq and Kuwait.

1884
01:11:31.240 --> 01:11:36.420
And she went to the smaller bases that a lot of people didn't go to that kind of rarely get entertainment.

1885
01:11:37.100 --> 01:11:37.680
Love that.

1886
01:11:37.880 --> 01:11:38.520
Love you.

1887
01:11:38.680 --> 01:11:39.200
Wendy.

1888
01:11:39.800 --> 01:11:40.760
Thank you so much.

1889
01:11:40.820 --> 01:11:42.440
It was so great to reconnect with you.

1890
01:11:42.600 --> 01:11:44.720
Thanks for sharing your memories of the office.

1891
01:11:44.960 --> 01:11:47.680
You guys, you can find Wendy in so many places.

1892
01:11:47.680 --> 01:11:48.700
She's so talented.

1893
01:11:48.960 --> 01:11:51.600
She has a podcast called Generation Ripe.

1894
01:11:51.860 --> 01:11:54.200
It is for teenagers over 40.

1895
01:11:54.780 --> 01:12:00.960
It's described as a Generation Ripe listener is someone who thinks of their age as an asset, not a liability.

1896
01:12:01.280 --> 01:12:05.140
We are older people with young souls are younger people with old souls.

1897
01:12:05.700 --> 01:12:07.540
Of course, she's on the Goldbergs.

1898
01:12:07.640 --> 01:12:09.020
They're about to start season nine.

1899
01:12:09.300 --> 01:12:14.660
She's on a Marvel series on Hulu called Modak and just tons of other things, you guys.

1900
01:12:14.820 --> 01:12:19.060
And you can find her on Instagram at Wendy McClendon Covey.

1901
01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:20.820
Let's see.

1902
01:12:20.980 --> 01:12:21.980
Where should we go?

1903
01:12:22.120 --> 01:12:23.600
It's the next day.

1904
01:12:24.200 --> 01:12:25.740
Oh, people are hungover.

1905
01:12:25.900 --> 01:12:27.200
Very hungover.

1906
01:12:27.980 --> 01:12:30.820
Andy does not remember calling Angela.

1907
01:12:31.120 --> 01:12:32.020
I know.

1908
01:12:32.440 --> 01:12:33.140
Oh, no.

1909
01:12:33.960 --> 01:12:34.440
Oh.

1910
01:12:34.700 --> 01:12:37.300
Michael is very melancholy.

1911
01:12:37.560 --> 01:12:37.800
Yeah.

1912
01:12:37.800 --> 01:12:43.560
And he has to go sell paper at Catalyst Paper, which I looked it up.

1913
01:12:43.720 --> 01:12:43.940
Yeah.

1914
01:12:44.460 --> 01:12:53.800
It's a real Canadian paper company, but it looks like its headquarters is based in Richmond, British Columbia, which is over on the West CoaSt.

1915
01:12:54.100 --> 01:12:56.640
I could not find a Winnipeg branch.

1916
01:12:56.920 --> 01:12:59.300
I mean, if you couldn't, I doubt it's there.

1917
01:12:59.300 --> 01:13:03.820
David Wallace calls to congratulate Michael because he did it.

1918
01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:04.960
He got the sale.

1919
01:13:05.560 --> 01:13:07.020
Michael is mad.

1920
01:13:07.300 --> 01:13:13.700
Yeah, because he's realized that this trip was a band-aid to try to help his feelings about Holly being transferred.

1921
01:13:13.960 --> 01:13:14.920
He's upset.

1922
01:13:14.920 --> 01:13:16.600
And he tells him off.

1923
01:13:16.740 --> 01:13:16.960
Yeah.

1924
01:13:17.200 --> 01:13:18.920
I have a location breakdown.

1925
01:13:19.880 --> 01:13:21.200
Why do you say it like that?

1926
01:13:21.580 --> 01:13:22.220
I don't know.

1927
01:13:22.440 --> 01:13:23.380
You said it like sad sack.

1928
01:13:23.620 --> 01:13:24.600
You said it like this.

1929
01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:26.360
I have a location breakdown.

1930
01:13:26.360 --> 01:13:28.040
Well, I'm sad with Michael.

1931
01:13:28.340 --> 01:13:28.620
Aw.

1932
01:13:28.960 --> 01:13:29.240
Yeah.

1933
01:13:29.640 --> 01:13:33.940
For the Winnipeg Airport for these scenes, we used Terminal No.

1934
01:13:34.100 --> 01:13:35.700
2 at LAX.

1935
01:13:36.040 --> 01:13:43.760
Randy let me know that there was an area that was out of service at that time and that our location manager, Kyle Alexander,

1936
01:13:44.260 --> 01:13:48.360
was able to get an agreement from the LAX airport to let us film there.

1937
01:13:48.580 --> 01:13:49.800
That's like unheard of.

1938
01:13:49.940 --> 01:13:52.040
Well, LAX does not let you film.

1939
01:13:52.240 --> 01:13:53.040
Like, they are hardcore.

1940
01:13:53.040 --> 01:13:58.480
Well, especially after 9-11, they had incredibly strict airport security.

1941
01:13:58.700 --> 01:14:04.760
So we were only allowed a very small number of cast and crew and extras.

1942
01:14:05.140 --> 01:14:10.640
Everybody had to submit credentials very far in advance and had to be screened.

1943
01:14:10.760 --> 01:14:14.660
So he said they had to create what they called a micro unit.

1944
01:14:15.180 --> 01:14:16.760
That's how small it was.

1945
01:14:16.960 --> 01:14:22.440
He said that the airplanes outside the window were not Air Canada planes.

1946
01:14:23.040 --> 01:14:29.240
They had to paint over the logos using visual effects during post-production.

1947
01:14:29.240 --> 01:14:33.080
And so they put the red maple leaf on the tail of one of the planes.

1948
01:14:33.620 --> 01:14:35.660
We could only afford to do one plane.

1949
01:14:36.040 --> 01:14:38.820
If you look over Michael's shoulder, it's on the left.

1950
01:14:38.940 --> 01:14:39.760
Yeah, I saw it.

1951
01:14:40.580 --> 01:14:48.900
Well, before we get to this big business with Jim and Pam, we should probably let you know that Kelly is going to break up with Daryl.

1952
01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:51.440
I mean, Ryan has a text ready to go.

1953
01:14:51.540 --> 01:14:53.100
They just have to hit send together.

1954
01:14:53.520 --> 01:14:55.740
And I think, what does Ryan want?

1955
01:14:55.880 --> 01:14:58.460
Does he want Daryl to come upstairs and make a scene?

1956
01:14:58.640 --> 01:14:59.700
What does Ryan want?

1957
01:14:59.860 --> 01:15:02.160
He wants to be the other guy.

1958
01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:05.360
Yeah, he wants a reaction out of Daryl.

1959
01:15:05.660 --> 01:15:07.440
Daryl seems relieved.

1960
01:15:07.440 --> 01:15:09.940
He's whistling as he walks to his car.

1961
01:15:10.120 --> 01:15:11.880
He responds immediately, okay, cool.

1962
01:15:11.880 --> 01:15:15.300
And then literally is like doing a dance to his pickup truck.

1963
01:15:15.480 --> 01:15:15.700
Yeah.

1964
01:15:15.880 --> 01:15:18.420
He's like, whew, thank you, Ryan.

1965
01:15:18.780 --> 01:15:23.880
And that then, of course, immediately makes Ryan less interested in this relationship.

1966
01:15:24.140 --> 01:15:24.720
And here we go.

1967
01:15:24.880 --> 01:15:25.380
And they're back.

1968
01:15:25.380 --> 01:15:25.780
Here we go.

1969
01:15:26.660 --> 01:15:27.420
All right.

1970
01:15:27.860 --> 01:15:29.320
Jim is going to leave.

1971
01:15:29.320 --> 01:15:33.440
And again, you guys have this Jim and Pam scene at that golden hour.

1972
01:15:33.840 --> 01:15:36.280
Your hair is sparkling with sunshine.

1973
01:15:36.760 --> 01:15:39.360
It's like that sunset scene in the parking lot.

1974
01:15:39.460 --> 01:15:40.520
You couldn't look cuter.

1975
01:15:40.780 --> 01:15:41.360
I know.

1976
01:15:41.540 --> 01:15:43.580
I thought this scene was really sweet.

1977
01:15:44.180 --> 01:15:44.580
Yeah.

1978
01:15:44.780 --> 01:15:47.120
Pam is standing in the parking lot.

1979
01:15:47.300 --> 01:15:49.540
And Jim says, what are you doing here?

1980
01:15:49.900 --> 01:15:54.780
Because he had told her on the phone, when you come back, you're going to come back the right way.

1981
01:15:55.060 --> 01:15:55.400
Right.

1982
01:15:55.500 --> 01:15:56.240
And he's supportive.

1983
01:15:56.240 --> 01:15:58.980
If she wants to take the class again, they'll get through it.

1984
01:15:58.980 --> 01:15:59.260
Yes.

1985
01:15:59.400 --> 01:16:04.680
That the right way to come back is for her to come back having graduated from art school.

1986
01:16:05.100 --> 01:16:06.680
So Pam's standing there.

1987
01:16:06.680 --> 01:16:09.520
And she says, I'm coming back the wrong way.

1988
01:16:09.860 --> 01:16:11.400
But she's OK with it.

1989
01:16:11.460 --> 01:16:12.660
She's happy about it.

1990
01:16:12.780 --> 01:16:13.920
She missed Scranton.

1991
01:16:14.080 --> 01:16:14.820
She missed Jim.

1992
01:16:15.160 --> 01:16:18.580
She actually hated the graphic design school.

1993
01:16:19.040 --> 01:16:19.480
Yeah.

1994
01:16:19.740 --> 01:16:23.340
She tells Jim, this isn't really what I wanted to do.

1995
01:16:23.480 --> 01:16:25.380
But she had to go to find that out.

1996
01:16:25.520 --> 01:16:26.120
She did.

1997
01:16:26.120 --> 01:16:28.800
You know, we had a question from Evan L.

1998
01:16:29.020 --> 01:16:32.460
Why did the writers have Pam fail art school?

1999
01:16:32.760 --> 01:16:34.260
Well, that's a good question, Evan.

2000
01:16:34.700 --> 01:16:36.720
I asked Brent Forrester about it.

2001
01:16:36.840 --> 01:16:44.740
He said that the story of Pam failing art school and coming back, quote, the wrong way, was a pitch from Jen Salata.

2002
01:16:45.280 --> 01:16:47.580
Brent said, I have a daughter.

2003
01:16:47.580 --> 01:16:50.520
And he said, I'm a big, fat feminiSt.

2004
01:16:50.620 --> 01:16:51.440
That's a quote.

2005
01:16:51.980 --> 01:16:57.760
And the idea that Pam would give up on her dreams to be with a man really rubbed him the wrong way.

2006
01:16:57.760 --> 01:17:05.860
But that Jen Salata absolutely insisted that the romantic power of this gesture outweighed any politics.

2007
01:17:05.860 --> 01:17:19.960
And also that the truth for Pam was not that she was giving up on her dreams for Jim, but that she was distinguishing what her real dreams were.

2008
01:17:19.960 --> 01:17:31.600
And I really liked that because I think what Pam did here in going to graphic design school was that she was taking her real dream of being a creative artiSt.

2009
01:17:31.600 --> 01:17:37.840
And she was doing what a lot of artists do, and she was trying to find a practical application.

2010
01:17:38.440 --> 01:17:38.580
Right.

2011
01:17:38.700 --> 01:17:40.660
How can I sort of do art but make a living?

2012
01:17:40.840 --> 01:17:41.160
Yeah.

2013
01:17:41.240 --> 01:17:41.420
Right?

2014
01:17:41.720 --> 01:17:42.920
Yes, exactly.

2015
01:17:43.280 --> 01:17:49.120
But, you know, I mean, people go into graphic design when they're passionate about graphic design.

2016
01:17:49.200 --> 01:17:51.300
It's not some backup job, right?

2017
01:17:51.600 --> 01:17:56.580
And the truth is, is that Pam discovered she does not have a passion for graphic design.

2018
01:17:56.580 --> 01:18:02.140
And that's sort of what I had in my head when I was doing that scene on the phone with Jim and Pam's crying.

2019
01:18:02.640 --> 01:18:07.420
Pam is realizing that even if she did pass the course, this isn't for her.

2020
01:18:07.900 --> 01:18:10.200
But she's also embarrassed that she failed.

2021
01:18:10.900 --> 01:18:17.540
But what she definitely does not want to do is spend 12 more weeks studying something she doesn't love.

2022
01:18:17.880 --> 01:18:22.080
For me in that moment, I just saw Pam stating her truth.

2023
01:18:22.300 --> 01:18:24.480
And I always think that's so powerful.

2024
01:18:24.960 --> 01:18:25.120
Yeah.

2025
01:18:25.120 --> 01:18:29.860
There is a very sweet deleted scene, you guys, with Jim and Pam.

2026
01:18:30.080 --> 01:18:31.360
It's in the break room.

2027
01:18:31.520 --> 01:18:32.540
And you know what?

2028
01:18:32.640 --> 01:18:33.540
I'm titling it.

2029
01:18:33.820 --> 01:18:34.240
What?

2030
01:18:34.480 --> 01:18:35.720
Love in the break room.

2031
01:18:35.980 --> 01:18:36.920
What is it?

2032
01:18:37.460 --> 01:18:39.880
Pam is showing Jim her art assignment.

2033
01:18:40.160 --> 01:18:44.960
She has this portfolio, and she's showing him the piece that she got an F on.

2034
01:18:45.220 --> 01:18:47.640
Oh, I remember shooting this now.

2035
01:18:47.780 --> 01:18:48.320
Yes.

2036
01:18:48.480 --> 01:18:48.760
Yes.

2037
01:18:48.960 --> 01:18:50.620
Oh, I remember it.

2038
01:18:50.660 --> 01:18:51.840
It's really sweet.

2039
01:18:51.840 --> 01:18:58.260
And as he goes through her art assignments, in the back, tucked in the binder, is a sketch pad.

2040
01:18:58.760 --> 01:19:00.280
And he's like, what is this?

2041
01:19:00.380 --> 01:19:04.220
And she said, oh, you know, it's just sketches of things I would see in New York.

2042
01:19:04.900 --> 01:19:11.520
And when you look at them, Jenna, and I'll put it in stories, it's like people sitting at a cafe, things she might have seen on her walk.

2043
01:19:11.700 --> 01:19:13.100
And they're really good.

2044
01:19:13.520 --> 01:19:15.500
And this is such a wonderful moment.

2045
01:19:15.720 --> 01:19:17.080
I wish it had stayed in.

2046
01:19:17.460 --> 01:19:18.640
Sam, can you play the clip?

2047
01:19:19.720 --> 01:19:20.640
This is my project.

2048
01:19:21.660 --> 01:19:23.540
Before we start, how did you fail?

2049
01:19:24.060 --> 01:19:25.100
You're good at art.

2050
01:19:26.240 --> 01:19:26.640
See?

2051
01:19:27.160 --> 01:19:27.560
Yeah.

2052
01:19:27.760 --> 01:19:29.580
We had to make a logo for a coffee company.

2053
01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:30.160
Well, that's nice.

2054
01:19:30.360 --> 01:19:31.320
This is the one that got an A.

2055
01:19:31.400 --> 01:19:32.400
The teacher handed it out.

2056
01:19:32.420 --> 01:19:32.680
Mm-hmm.

2057
01:19:32.860 --> 01:19:34.000
And this is mine.

2058
01:19:34.440 --> 01:19:34.960
Well, that's cool.

2059
01:19:35.200 --> 01:19:37.300
I couldn't get it to print out right, so I got an F.

2060
01:19:37.760 --> 01:19:38.680
I would have given you a D.

2061
01:19:39.160 --> 01:19:40.980
That's only because we were sleeping with the professor.

2062
01:19:42.220 --> 01:19:43.040
These are cool.

2063
01:19:44.540 --> 01:19:45.200
What's this?

2064
01:19:45.880 --> 01:19:46.900
That's my sketchbook.

2065
01:19:47.300 --> 01:19:49.360
It's just, you know, stuff I saw in New York.

2066
01:19:49.560 --> 01:19:50.240
People on the subway.

2067
01:19:50.900 --> 01:19:51.300
Whoa.

2068
01:19:52.200 --> 01:19:53.580
This is great.

2069
01:19:53.940 --> 01:19:55.880
It's so much different than your other work.

2070
01:19:56.640 --> 01:19:57.660
Wow, Pam.

2071
01:19:58.580 --> 01:19:59.900
That is awesome.

2072
01:20:00.640 --> 01:20:01.120
Yeah?

2073
01:20:03.220 --> 01:20:03.700
Yeah.

2074
01:20:05.120 --> 01:20:06.720
Oh, I wish that had stayed in.

2075
01:20:06.720 --> 01:20:13.820
It's a really sweet scene, and I feel like it just shows what Pam did get out of New York,

2076
01:20:13.820 --> 01:20:16.000
and I really wish it had been in the episode.

2077
01:20:16.180 --> 01:20:20.820
I agree, because I don't think Pam's art story is over yet.

2078
01:20:21.020 --> 01:20:21.420
Mm-hmm.

2079
01:20:21.420 --> 01:20:30.600
You know, she did not have the benefit of, I think, parents or role models who encouraged

2080
01:20:30.600 --> 01:20:37.620
her to follow this piece of herself, and she only finally does now have that person in Jim.

2081
01:20:37.800 --> 01:20:38.160
Mm-hmm.

2082
01:20:38.260 --> 01:20:43.440
So she's going to have to take her art journey at the same time as her romantic journey.

2083
01:20:43.800 --> 01:20:47.160
Well, I'm going to put that scene in stories, because it's really sweet.

2084
01:20:47.320 --> 01:20:48.300
I loved it.

2085
01:20:48.400 --> 01:20:49.080
I did, too.

2086
01:20:49.080 --> 01:20:51.520
Well, that kind of wraps up this episode.

2087
01:20:51.800 --> 01:20:52.120
Yeah.

2088
01:20:52.460 --> 01:20:55.380
Thank you to everyone who helped us make this episode.

2089
01:20:55.560 --> 01:20:55.800
Mm-hmm.

2090
01:20:55.960 --> 01:21:02.280
Brent Forrester, Randy Cordray, Wendy McClendon-Covey, and Rich Sommer, thanks for coming on for

2091
01:21:02.280 --> 01:21:02.840
an interview.

2092
01:21:03.120 --> 01:21:04.360
It takes a village.

2093
01:21:04.520 --> 01:21:04.960
It does.

2094
01:21:05.120 --> 01:21:09.320
And thank you to you guys for sending in awesome questions and Winnipeg trivia.

2095
01:21:09.700 --> 01:21:10.120
Yes.

2096
01:21:10.360 --> 01:21:12.120
Some last parting advice.

2097
01:21:12.520 --> 01:21:17.000
According to Andy, Long Island iced teas are way stronger in Canada.

2098
01:21:17.000 --> 01:21:20.140
So be careful the next time you order one of those.

2099
01:21:20.480 --> 01:21:21.040
Yes.

2100
01:21:21.580 --> 01:21:22.760
All right, you guys.

2101
01:21:23.000 --> 01:21:26.540
Well, that was Business Trip, and we will be seeing you next week.

2102
01:21:26.740 --> 01:21:28.040
Yeah, see you next week.

2103
01:21:31.550 --> 01:21:34.030
